    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d432">

                <pb facs="0451" n="447" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg447"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.943 Manchuria/53: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-03T22:00:00+08:00">March 3, 1938—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 3—1:58 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>354. Following from Tokyo.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“March 3, 6 p.m. Reference Department’s 319, November 28, 3 p.m.,<anchor
                            n="94" xml:id="d432-f1"/> extraterritoriality in Manchuria.</p>
                    <pb facs="0452" n="448" xml:id="pg448"/>
                    <p>The Foreign Minister has replied to my note of December 2 [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">1?</hi>], 1937<anchor n="96" xml:id="d432-f2"/> (see
                        despatch No. 2689 of December 9, 1937<anchor n="97" xml:id="d432-f3"/>)
                        under date of March l.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d432-f4"/> The Foreign
                        Minister’s note in translation refers to the subject matter of my note and
                        concludes with the following paragraph. <quote rend="blockquote">
                            <p>‘The policy of the Government of Manchukuo in this instance, which
                                concerns the treatment of nationals of third countries and juridical
                                persons of third countries, is a matter in which the Japanese
                                Government is not concerned and the Japanese Government,
                                accordingly, regrets that it is not in a position to make any
                                explanation’.</p>
                        </quote></p>
                    <p>Please repeat to Peiping, Hankow and to the Department as our No. 146, March
                        3, 6 p.m. Grew”.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
